verses.text:
“On that day,” declares Yahweh,
“you will call me ‘My husband,’
and no longer will you call me ‘My Baal.’{f}

I will betroth you to me forever;
I will betroth you to me in righteousness and in justice,
in steadfast love and in compassion.

“On that day I will answer,” declares Yahweh,
“I will answer the heavens,
and they will answer the earth;

I will sow her for myself in the land;
on Lo-ruhamah I will have mercy,
and to Lo-ammi I will say, ‘You are my people’;
and he shall say, ‘My God.’ ”{h}

Yet let no one bring a charge,
let no one rebuke;
for with you is my contention, O priest.{b}

They will eat, but not be satisfied;
they will play the whore, but not multiply;
for they have abandoned Yahweh
to devote themselves

My people ask advice of their wooden thing,
and their rod answers them;
for a spirit of harlotry has misled them,
and they have played the harlot out from under their God.

I will not punish your daughters for their harlotry,
or your daughters-in-law for their adulteries;
for the men themselves go off with harlots
and offer sacrifices with the shrine women;{e}
so a people that does not discern is thrown down.

Though you, Israel, play the harlot,
do not let Judah incur guilt.
Do not come to Gilgal;
do not go up to Beth-aven;{f}
do not swear, “As Yahweh lives.”

Hear this, you priests!
Pay heed, house of Israel!
Give ear, house of the king!
For the judgment concerns you;
for you have been a snare at Mizpah,
and a net spread out upon Tabor.

I know Ephraim,{b}
and Israel is not hidden from me;
for now, Ephraim, you have played the harlot;
Israel is defiled.

Their deeds do not allow them
to return to their God;
for a spirit of harlotry is within them,
and Yahweh they do not know.

The pride of Israel testifies to his face;
Israel and Ephraim shall stumble in their iniquity;
Judah also shall stumble with them.

With their flocks and their herds they shall go
to seek Yahweh, but they shall not find him;
he has withdrawn from them.

Toward Yahweh they have been treacherous,
for they have fathered children of strangers.
Now a new moon will eat them up, them and their portions.{c}

The princes of Judah have become
like those who shift the boundary stone;
on them I will pour out my wrath like water.

I will go back to my place
until they acknowledge their guilt and seek my face;
in their distress they will search for me earnestly.

“Come, let us return to Yahweh;
for he has torn, and he will heal us;
he has struck, and he will bind us up.

After two days he will revive us;
on the third day he will raise us up,{a}
that we may live before him.

Let us know, let us press on to know Yahweh;
his going out is as certain as the dawn;
he will come to us like the showers,
like the spring rain that waters the earth.”

Therefore I have hewn them by the prophets;
I have slain them by the words of my mouth,
and my judgment goes out like light.{b}

As robbers lie in wait for a man,
so a band of priests murders on the road to Shechem;
indeed they commit villainy.

When I would bring healing to Israel,
then Ephraim’s guilt comes to light,
and Samaria’s wicked acts;
for they deal in falsehood;
thieves break in,
and raiders plunder in the streets.

For they draw near; their heart is like an oven in their ambush;
all night their anger{a} sleeps;
in the morning it blazes like a flaming fire.

Every one of them is hot like an oven;
they consume those who judge them.{b}
Their kings fall, one after another,
and not one among them calls to me.

The pride of Israel testifies to his face;
yet they do not return to Yahweh their God,
nor seek him for all this.

Ephraim has become like a dove,
gullible and senseless;
to Egypt they call, to Assyria they go.

Wherever they go I will cast my net upon them;
like birds of the sky I will pull them down;
I will chasten them as their congregation has heard.{c}

They do not call to me with their heart
when they howl on their beds;
for the sake of grain and wine they slash themselves;{d}
they turn their backs on me.

Your calf is rejected, Samaria;{b}
my anger burns against them.
How long will they be incapable of innocence?

For they sow the wind,
and they shall reap the whirlwind.
The standing grain has no head;
it shall yield no flour;
and if it should yield,
strangers would devour it.

Israel is swallowed up;
already they are among the nations
like a vessel no one wants.

For they have gone up to Assyria,
a wild donkey wandering alone;
Ephraim has hired lovers.

As for my sacrificial gifts,
they sacrifice meat and eat it;
Yahweh does not accept them.
Now he will remember their iniquity
and punish their sins;
they shall return to Egypt.

They will not stay on Yahweh’s land;
Ephraim will go back to Egypt,
and eat what is unclean in Assyria.

They shall not pour out wine to Yahweh,
and their sacrifices shall not please him.
It shall be to them like mourners’ bread;
all who eat of it shall be defiled;
for their bread shall be for their hunger only;
it shall not come into the house of Yahweh.

For look, they have gone, fleeing from ruin;
Egypt will gather them up,
Memphis{a} will bury them.
Their treasures of silver will be overrun by nettles;
brambles will grow in their tents.

Ephraim is a watchman with my God—the prophet;
yet a bird-catcher’s trap lies on all his paths,
and enmity in the house of his God.

They have deeply corrupted themselves
as in the days of Gibeah;{b}
he will remember their iniquity;
he will punish their sins.

All their evil is at Gilgal;{e}
there I came to hate them.
Because of the wickedness of their deeds
I will drive them out of my house.
I will love them no more;
all their princes are rebels.

Ephraim is stricken;
their root is dried up;
they shall bear no fruit.
Even though they give birth,
I will put to death their cherished offspring.

My God will cast them off,
for they have not obeyed him,
and among the nations they will be wanderers.

Israel is a rambling vine{a}
that puts forth fruit for itself.
As his fruit grew plentiful,
so he added altars;
as his land grew good,
so he made his pillars handsome.

They speak mere words;
with empty oaths they cut covenants;
so judgment springs up like poisonous weeds
in the furrows of the field.

Since the days of Gibeah you have sinned, Israel;
there they have stood fast.{i}
Will not war overtake them in Gibeah,
against the children of injustice?

When I please, I will chasten them,
and peoples will be gathered against them
when they are bound for their double iniquity.{j}

Ephraim was a heifer well taught,
fond of threshing,
and I passed over her fair neck;{k}
but now I will harness Ephraim;
Judah shall drag the plow;
Jacob shall break the clods for himself.

Sow for yourselves righteousness;
reap the fruit of steadfast love;
break up your untilled ground,
for it is time to seek Yahweh,
until he comes
and rains righteousness upon you.{l}

You have plowed wickedness;
you have reaped injustice;
you have eaten falsehood’s fruit.
Since you relied on your own way,{m}
on your multitude of warriors,

But I myself taught Ephraim to walk,
lifting them in my{d} arms;
yet they never knew that it was I who healed them.

With human cords I led them,
with ropes of love;
I was to them as one who eases the yoke on their jaws,{e}
and I bent down to them and fed them.

They will not{f} return to the land of Egypt;
but Assyria will be their king,
because they refuse to turn back.

The sword will whirl through their cities;
it will make an end of their gate bars{g}
and devour them because of their schemes.

My people are bent on turning from me;
though they call to the Most High,{h}
he will by no means raise them up.

How can I give you up, Ephraim?
How can I hand you over, Israel?
How can I make you like Admah?
How can I treat you like Zeboiim?{i}
My heart turns over within me;
all my compassion grows warm and tender.

I will not act on my burning anger;
I will not turn back to destroy Ephraim;
for I am God and not a man,
the Holy One in your midst,
and I will not come in wrath.{j}

They will walk after Yahweh;
he will roar like a lion;
when he roars,
his children will come trembling from the west.

Like birds they will come trembling out of Egypt,
and like doves from the land of Assyria;
and I will settle them in their homes, declares Yahweh.

Ephraim has surrounded me with lies,{k}
and the house of Israel with deceit;
but Judah still wanders with God,{l}
and is faithful to the Holy One.{m}

Ephraim grazes on the wind,{a}
and chases the east wind all day long;
he multiplies falsehood and violence;
they make a treaty with Assyria,
and oil is carried to Egypt.

Yahweh has a dispute with Judah too;
he will punish Jacob for his conduct
and pay him back for what he has done.

He strove with the angel and prevailed;
he wept and sought his favor.
At Bethel he found him,{c}
and there he spoke with us{d}—

As for you, return to your God;
keep steadfast love and justice,
and wait on your God continually.

Ephraim has said, “Ah, but I have grown rich;
I have gained wealth for myself;
in all my labors they will find in me
no iniquity that is sin.”

I am Yahweh your God
since the land of Egypt;
once more I will make you dwell in tents,
as in the days of the festival.{h}

I spoke through the prophets;
I gave vision upon vision,
and through the prophets I spoke in likenesses.

If Gilead is wickedness,
they are surely worthless;
in Gilgal they sacrifice bulls;
their altars also will be like stone heaps
on the furrows of the field.{i}

Jacob fled to the open country of Aram;{j}
there Israel served for a wife,
and for a wife he kept sheep.

Ephraim has given bitter provocation;
therefore his Lord will let his blood remain upon him
and pay him back for his contempt.

Once when Ephraim spoke, men trembled;
he was lifted high in Israel;
but he became guilty through Baal, and he died.

Now they sin more and more;
they make themselves cast images,
idols from their silver according to their skill,
every one a craftsman’s product.
Of these they say,
“Men who sacrifice kiss calves!”{a}

But I am Yahweh your God
from the land of Egypt;
you know no God but me,
and besides me there is no savior.{c}

As they grazed, they were sated;
sated, their hearts grew proud;
and so they forgot me.

Like a bear bereft of her young I will meet them
and rip apart the covering of their heart;{e}
there like a lioness I will consume them;
the beast of the field shall tear them.

Shall I ransom them from the power of Sheol?{h}
Shall I redeem them from Death?
O Death, where are your plagues?
O Sheol, where is your sting?{i}
Compassion is hidden from my eyes.

Samaria will bear her guilt,{k}
because she has rebelled against her God;
they will fall by the sword;
their little ones will be dashed in pieces,
and their pregnant women ripped open.

Assyria will not save us;
we will not ride on horses;
and we will no more say ‘Our God’
to the work of our hands.
In you the orphan finds mercy.”

I will heal their faithlessness;
I will love them freely,
for my anger has turned away from them.

I will be like the dew to Israel;
he will blossom like the lily
and strike root like the trees of Lebanon.{c}

His shoots will spread;
his splendor will be like the olive,
and his fragrance like Lebanon.

Those who live in his shade will return;
they will grow grain{d}
and blossom like the vine;
their renown will be like the wine of Lebanon.

Ephraim, what have I to do with idols anymore?{e}
I answer him, I look after him.
I am like an evergreen cypress;
your fruit is found from me.
